  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 229 AM CDT Wed Sep 2 2026\n\nScattered thunderstorms developed across Winnebago, Calumet and \nManitowoc counties early this morning in response to 850mb \nwarm advection. Some of the stronger storms could produce small\nhail, especially across Waushara County through 3 am. Meanwhile, \nadditional convection was noted across west-central WI with\ncomplex of severe thunderstorms across southeast Minnesota where \na gust to 59 knots was recorded. This complex of storms is\nexpected to move into central WI by 5 am. Some of the storms could\nproduce strong/damaging winds and hail up to one inch in diameter. \n\nThe showers and storms come to an end later this morning with dry\nconditions expected this afternoon. A break in the storms is\nexpected Wednesday evening, with the next round of storms expected\nlate Wednesday night and Thursday. Timing and location hard to\npinpoint between the differing model solutions. The latest NAM \nhas trended southward which seems reasonable with the best\ninstability to our south. The Storm Prediction Center Day 2\nSevere Storm Outlook has a Marginal (Level 1 of 5) or Slight \n(Level 2 of 5) south of a Merill to Manitowoc line. From Friday\nthrough the weekend, there are periodic chances of thunderstorms\nwhile above normal temperatures continue into next week.",
